From 4a46f645864ead6ed8d0ae7c17e5659d9ec29a7f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 08:47:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/9] Desktop host: the worker yields with an immediate, so
 that a hold reaches it while the run is going

The throttled-link measurement found that the flow control did not act: the worker continued its run through a MessageChannel of its own, Node took the
messages of that port in one go (up to 1000 in a turn, those the handler posts to itself included), parentPort was not read in between, and the host's
hold reached the worker only when the run was over. 64 MiB of snapshots were produced after the host had said hold at 11 MiB, a ping waited behind all of
them (54 MiB ahead of it at 20 Mbit/s with a 4 MiB hold), and a pause was answered when the run ended. With an immediate between chunks the event loop turns
and the hold is read within a chunk (40 ms); the unthrottled run takes as long as before (three runs each, alternating: 2.8, 3.4 and 3.6 s against 2.8, 3.3 and
3.9 s on a loaded machine). A test with the real engine holds the run after four snapshots and counts what is produced in the next 1.5 s: 24 with the old yield,
at most 4 now. linkMeasure.ts also records how many bytes stood ahead of each ping.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016zPE7K49ycKieo7wJdrBFN
